toolCallRounds.response:
Likely cause: the client process gets killed by SIGPIPE when it attempts to send on a socket whose peer (the SS) has died and the socket is broken. send()/write() to a closed TCP socket can raise SIGPIPE by default — that terminates the process. Your code does not ignore SIGPIPE nor use MSG_NOSIGNAL, so a broken SS connection leads to the client exiting as you described.

What to do (two small fixes):
1. Ignore SIGPIPE in the client so a send() returns -1 and you can handle the error instead of getting terminated.
2. Use MSG_NOSIGNAL in send() inside [`send_message`](common.c) (or handle SIGPIPE centrally). Also make send_message robust to EINTR and return -1 on real send errors so callers can reconnect/cleanup.

I recommend both changes; below are minimal patches.

- Files referenced:
  - [`handle_write`](client.c) — [client.c](client.c)
  - [`send_message`](common.c) — [common.c](common.c)
  - heartbeat code: [`heartbeat_thread`](ss.c) — [ss.c](ss.c)
  - SS connection handling: [`handle_ss_connection`](nm.c) — [nm.c](nm.c)

